ment for Elliott on the other hand  I think you know I hate to say it but  legally I'm not sure that matters  because under article 46 it's Goodell is  call as to what is the punishment and he  could have reached that decision on also  for all sorts of reasons so it's  meaningful from the standpoint of common  sense for public relations I'm not sure  if the  attaches the same level of meaning does  the fact that Elliot was never charged  with a crime or there's never any  adjudication help him at all in these  proceedings not really the fact of  Goodell doesn't need a criminal charge  to punish a player  I think it's meaningful in the sense  that it suggests that the case against  him isn't as strong as some believe but  it but in terms of does it authorize the  punishment does it impact whether or not  a punishment should be upheld on appeal  the answer to that is no speculate for  me here a bit Mike my issue with a lot  of this is that the NFL is acting as  investigator judge and jury on an  instant and an alleged crime that  effectively brands a guy for the rest of  his life I mean if this goes through in  Ezekiel it is suspended for multiple  games it'll always be a footnote in his  his life his career that he's been  branded a domestic abuser do you have  any problem with the NFL despite the  fact they did do a thorough  investigation conducting this process  the way that they did it I think the NFL  has the worst system for player conduct  matters if we look at the other lis I  mean just look at the NBA does the NBA  ever have anything like this the NBA  avoids right I mean it's a totally  different dynamic dynamic in the NBA  it's almost as if in the NFL there is an  invitation to have a legal fight over a  player suspension so I don't think this  is a good system at all I think a better  system would have someone neutral if a  suspension is after a certain length but  that's not what the players agree to and  at the end of the day there's a contract  and tha